WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.648 --> 00:00:04.978
- The notion that we are
not enough, is a lie.

00:00:04.978 --> 00:00:06.418
It's important for us to remember

00:00:06.418 --> 00:00:08.074
the joy in what we do.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:11.354
I was terrified to pursue
acting as a career.

00:00:11.354 --> 00:00:14.138
I really didn't think
that this was kind of

00:00:14.138 --> 00:00:18.306
an industry that I had what
it takes to compete in.

00:00:18.306 --> 00:00:20.476
- She's an American actress
who rose to prominence

00:00:20.476 --> 00:00:23.620
for starring in ABCs drama, Scandal.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.421
For her work in Scandal she was nominated

00:00:25.421 --> 00:00:28.545
for an Emmy Award at the 65th and 66th

00:00:28.545 --> 00:00:30.385
primetime Emmy Awards.

00:00:30.385 --> 00:00:33.182
In 2014, Time Magazine included her

00:00:33.182 --> 00:00:35.360
in their Time 100 list.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.523
She's Kerry Washington,

00:00:36.523 --> 00:00:39.327
and here's my take on her
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:39.327 --> 00:00:40.888
Rule number one is a personal favorite.

00:00:40.888 --> 00:00:42.569
I'd make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:42.569 --> 00:00:43.973
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:43.973 --> 00:00:45.162
Also, as Kerry is talking,

00:00:45.162 --> 00:00:47.184
if she says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:47.184 --> 00:00:48.736
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:48.736 --> 00:00:49.766
and put quotes around it,

00:00:49.766 --> 00:00:52.049
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:52.049 --> 00:00:52.882
Enjoy!

00:00:56.126 --> 00:00:58.626
(light music)

00:01:06.328 --> 00:01:10.963
- The notion that we are
not enough, is a lie.

00:01:10.963 --> 00:01:13.300
And I think more young
people need to know that.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.728
That like when you have that thought

00:01:15.728 --> 00:01:18.112
that you're not enough,

00:01:18.112 --> 00:01:20.197
that that's based on a lie

00:01:20.197 --> 00:01:21.714
that somebody said to you,

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:25.024
or a lie that somebody did to you.

00:01:25.024 --> 00:01:26.823
Like somebody said or did something to you

00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:29.842
to make you feel like you're not enough,

00:01:29.842 --> 00:01:31.933
and that's not true.

00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:35.600
That you are everything
that you need to be.

00:01:36.613 --> 00:01:39.211
That you have everything
that you need to have

00:01:39.211 --> 00:01:41.346
to be the person that you're meant to be,

00:01:41.346 --> 00:01:44.887
and to be the best version
of who you're meant to be.

00:01:44.887 --> 00:01:47.509
It may mean that you
have to work really hard,

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:50.247
and it may mean that you
have to ask for help,

00:01:50.247 --> 00:01:52.747
but you are perfectly blessed.

00:01:54.841 --> 00:01:58.438
You know, you are exactly
who you're supposed to be

00:01:58.438 --> 00:02:01.120
to be joyful in this life.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.797
So don't be afraid to work hard,

00:02:02.797 --> 00:02:04.116
and don't be afraid to ask for help

00:02:04.116 --> 00:02:05.304
so you can get there.

00:02:05.304 --> 00:02:07.357
'Cause that's your truth.

00:02:07.357 --> 00:02:10.357
I had a yoga teacher who said to me,

00:02:11.627 --> 00:02:14.314
"Always stay on your own yoga mat.

00:02:14.314 --> 00:02:16.689
"Because sometimes if you're in headstand,

00:02:16.689 --> 00:02:18.165
"and you're too busy looking at the person

00:02:18.165 --> 00:02:18.998
"on the other mat,

00:02:18.998 --> 00:02:20.643
"you could literally break your neck."

00:02:20.643 --> 00:02:21.531
(laughs)

00:02:21.531 --> 00:02:24.403
I feel like that's a really
good metaphor for life.

00:02:24.403 --> 00:02:27.015
To stay on your own path,
do what's right for you.

00:02:27.015 --> 00:02:28.329
You can't compare your insides

00:02:28.329 --> 00:02:29.512
with somebody else's outsides,

00:02:29.512 --> 00:02:30.380
because you never know what's going on

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.845
for somebody else, really.

00:02:31.845 --> 00:02:33.059
- You don't have a lot of free time

00:02:33.059 --> 00:02:34.873
to do other stuff, do you?

00:02:34.873 --> 00:02:35.841
- No I don't.

00:02:35.841 --> 00:02:38.479
- Have there been situations
where there was something

00:02:38.479 --> 00:02:41.469
that you really wanted to do, movie wise,

00:02:41.469 --> 00:02:45.407
or any other part that you
just weren't able to do

00:02:45.407 --> 00:02:46.419
because of your schedule?

00:02:46.419 --> 00:02:49.157
- Yeah, it's come up a couple times.

00:02:49.157 --> 00:02:50.815
But that's the commitment you make,

00:02:50.815 --> 00:02:52.944
particularly when you're
doing a network show.

00:02:52.944 --> 00:02:54.550
You kind of have to be willing

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:55.751
to let some other stuff go.

00:02:55.751 --> 00:02:58.142
And it's helped me to really focus

00:02:58.142 --> 00:03:00.965
so that the projects
that I do during hiatus,

00:03:00.965 --> 00:03:02.964
like the movie I'm
going to do this season,

00:03:02.964 --> 00:03:05.338
is something I'm really,
really passionate about,

00:03:05.338 --> 00:03:07.797
because I only have one
window to do something else,

00:03:07.797 --> 00:03:10.355
so it has to be something
that means a lot to me.

00:03:10.355 --> 00:03:12.355
I got great advice from,

00:03:13.358 --> 00:03:16.010
one of the people that totally
fascinates me is Bob Iger,

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.852
he's the CEO of the Walt Disney ABC world,

00:03:19.852 --> 00:03:23.259
so his job is so weird to
me 'cause he is in charge

00:03:23.259 --> 00:03:25.271
of everything from like a Disney cruise

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:27.085
to George Stephanopoulos ABC News,

00:03:27.085 --> 00:03:30.532
like he, to like a theme park to Scandal,

00:03:30.532 --> 00:03:33.611
like all of that falls
into the ABC Disney family,

00:03:33.611 --> 00:03:36.065
and I asked him, and it
was very helpful to me,

00:03:36.065 --> 00:03:37.278
I asked him a few months ago,

00:03:37.278 --> 00:03:40.774
like how do you decide what
you're going to work on in a day?

00:03:40.774 --> 00:03:42.848
And he said something so valuable to me,

00:03:42.848 --> 00:03:43.767
so I want to share it with you guys.

00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:46.817
He said 80% of his time,

00:03:46.817 --> 00:03:49.752
he tries to think about
where can I have impact?

00:03:49.752 --> 00:03:51.658
Where am I going to make a difference

00:03:51.658 --> 00:03:53.283
in the day to day?

00:03:53.283 --> 00:03:55.898
So it may not usually be Disney cruises,

00:03:55.898 --> 00:03:57.826
but sometimes it needs to be,

00:03:57.826 --> 00:03:59.554
because the cruise business has been doing

00:03:59.554 --> 00:04:00.913
what the cruise business is doing.

00:04:00.913 --> 00:04:01.888
That's not quoting him, by the way.

00:04:01.888 --> 00:04:02.721
(laughter)

00:04:02.721 --> 00:04:05.663
But there, you know,
sometimes it's a Broadway show

00:04:05.663 --> 00:04:06.591
that's about to open,

00:04:06.591 --> 00:04:08.104
sometimes it's the ESPYs,

00:04:08.104 --> 00:04:10.438
'cause ESPN, that's where
his focus needs to be.

00:04:10.438 --> 00:04:13.219
So it's really about like
where can I have impact today,

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:14.833
and the other, where am I needed?

00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:18.438
The other 20% is what brings me joy.

00:04:18.438 --> 00:04:20.516
You know, so there are
certain movie projects

00:04:20.516 --> 00:04:24.141
that fall under the ABC Disney
world that bring him joy,

00:04:24.141 --> 00:04:27.440
or certain shows, or an opening of a park.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.787
There are certain things that bring him,

00:04:29.787 --> 00:04:31.235
that just fill him up

00:04:31.235 --> 00:04:33.749
that fall in his work responsibilities.

00:04:33.749 --> 00:04:37.122
And I just really have
tried to bring that with me

00:04:37.122 --> 00:04:40.432
because it's important for us to remember

00:04:40.432 --> 00:04:42.158
the joy in what we do.

00:04:42.158 --> 00:04:44.562
It's important to be
effective in our workplace,

00:04:44.562 --> 00:04:46.963
but it's important to
cultivate the moments

00:04:46.963 --> 00:04:48.593
that just bring us joy.

00:04:48.593 --> 00:04:50.761
Because that's how we'll shine

00:04:50.761 --> 00:04:53.241
in our lives as professionals.

00:04:53.241 --> 00:04:56.435
I joke around that I think
about quitting the business

00:04:56.435 --> 00:04:57.848
at least once a year.

00:04:57.848 --> 00:04:58.999
- Wow.

00:04:58.999 --> 00:05:01.141
- Because it's tough.

00:05:01.141 --> 00:05:04.480
And I know there's been
a lot of talk about

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.063
your misery being your message,

00:05:09.074 --> 00:05:12.686
and for me it has really
been that I have to be

00:05:12.686 --> 00:05:15.529
willing to go into the
places that scare me,

00:05:15.529 --> 00:05:17.784
in order for God to be able to use me.

00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:18.756
- Wow.

00:05:18.756 --> 00:05:22.958
- So that's often what my job asks of me,

00:05:22.958 --> 00:05:25.674
and sometimes I get tired
and talk about quitting,

00:05:25.674 --> 00:05:28.661
but then God puts another
wonderful project in front of me

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.453
and I think, alright,
I guess I'm back at it.

00:05:30.453 --> 00:05:32.092
- Here we go.
- Yeah.

00:05:32.092 --> 00:05:33.652
There was a period before Scandal

00:05:33.652 --> 00:05:36.706
when I went into the wilderness.

00:05:36.706 --> 00:05:39.198
And for me what that meant was,

00:05:39.198 --> 00:05:42.658
there was a lot of toxicity in my life.

00:05:42.658 --> 00:05:44.562
People that I needed to let go of

00:05:44.562 --> 00:05:48.378
in a personal way, in a business way.

00:05:48.378 --> 00:05:50.877
And also I felt like I
had been living my career

00:05:50.877 --> 00:05:52.538
kind of on auto pilot,

00:05:52.538 --> 00:05:56.121
and I took a break and
did a Broadway play,

00:05:56.969 --> 00:05:59.456
and I went back to my roots as an artist.

00:05:59.456 --> 00:06:02.240
And doing Broadway is not
going to make you rich,

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.920
I think it cost me money
to do the play on Broadway,

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.587
but it was my return
to my artistic essence,

00:06:12.447 --> 00:06:16.225
kind of the conversation
between audience and artist.

00:06:16.225 --> 00:06:20.392
And I worked with a director
who really challenged me.

00:06:22.016 --> 00:06:24.694
And so going into the
wilderness really meant

00:06:24.694 --> 00:06:28.861
stripping myself of a lot of
unnecessary weight in my life.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:33.898
And the ritual of doing theater is,

00:06:33.898 --> 00:06:36.082
I thought of it as going into a monastery,

00:06:36.082 --> 00:06:38.899
because you do the same
thing eight times a week,

00:06:38.899 --> 00:06:40.786
in the same place, you say the same words,

00:06:40.786 --> 00:06:42.441
with the same props,

00:06:42.441 --> 00:06:45.168
and so it is a ritual that you commit to

00:06:45.168 --> 00:06:48.237
and I just wanted to, in a monk-like way,

00:06:48.237 --> 00:06:52.239
commit to that ritual and
let it teach me about myself,

00:06:52.239 --> 00:06:53.885
and let myself unfold.

00:06:53.885 --> 00:06:57.117
And in that period I let
go of a lot of people,

00:06:57.117 --> 00:06:58.394
and a lot of things.

00:06:58.394 --> 00:07:01.050
I experienced a lot of
loss professionally.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.967
I had, for example, companies
that I was working with

00:07:02.967 --> 00:07:06.025
on an endorsement level
that walked away from me,

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:07.104
because not everybody loves,

00:07:07.104 --> 00:07:10.506
not everything is Broadway sexy.

00:07:10.506 --> 00:07:14.364
And my personal life changed a great deal.

00:07:14.364 --> 00:07:18.515
I allowed myself to be open
to change in my personal life.

00:07:18.515 --> 00:07:21.055
I don't talk about my
personal life very often,

00:07:21.055 --> 00:07:22.974
but that's true.

00:07:22.974 --> 00:07:26.960
So I think going into the
wilderness made me ready,

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.115
as an artist, as a person,
as a business woman,

00:07:30.115 --> 00:07:31.850
to receive Scandal.

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:33.837
- Who are your mentors in this business.

00:07:33.837 --> 00:07:36.671
I mean Shanda, obviously, you
guys have worked together,

00:07:36.671 --> 00:07:38.569
but backstage we were talking about,

00:07:38.569 --> 00:07:39.746
you met with Cicely Tyson.

00:07:39.746 --> 00:07:40.579
- Yeah.

00:07:40.579 --> 00:07:41.412
- And you were just like, oh!

00:07:41.412 --> 00:07:42.268
- Oh my gosh, yes.

00:07:42.268 --> 00:07:43.717
I mean I have heroes.

00:07:43.717 --> 00:07:46.599
Heroes of mine are,
you know, Cicely Tyson,

00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:50.373
and Diahann Carroll, Rita
Moreno, Barbra Streisand,

00:07:50.373 --> 00:07:52.670
I mean women who have just kind of said,

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:55.697
you know, oh I don't fit
your conventional idea

00:07:55.697 --> 00:07:57.470
of what a superstar looks like?

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:58.752
Too bad, you know.

00:07:58.752 --> 00:07:59.761
- I'm going to be one anyway.

00:07:59.761 --> 00:08:02.390
- Yeah exactly, I'm going to do me.

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:06.223
And those women have
been real heroes of mine.

00:08:08.678 --> 00:08:10.910
Mentors, Shanda's a big one,

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:13.325
Jane Fonda is a big mentor of mine,

00:08:13.325 --> 00:08:17.279
and she's somebody who
really beautifully combines

00:08:17.279 --> 00:08:19.375
her politics and activism with her work.

00:08:19.375 --> 00:08:21.398
I don't know how many people know this but

00:08:21.398 --> 00:08:25.610
the movie 9 to 5 resulted in
the union for secretaries.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:30.234
There had been no union for
administrative assistants,

00:08:30.234 --> 00:08:34.123
and that movie sort of
brought to light the need

00:08:34.123 --> 00:08:37.304
because of abuse in
the workplace of women.

00:08:37.304 --> 00:08:39.941
And that to me is always
a shining example of

00:08:39.941 --> 00:08:43.041
using art and really great, fun art,

00:08:43.041 --> 00:08:44.967
to better the world.

00:08:44.967 --> 00:08:46.183
So she's a real mentor of mine.

00:08:46.183 --> 00:08:50.202
Eva Ensler, who's the writer
of the Vagina Monologues.

00:08:50.202 --> 00:08:51.452
Yeah, um, yeah.

00:08:53.146 --> 00:08:57.937
If I can really show up for
life, with my imperfections,

00:08:57.937 --> 00:09:02.165
we give each other permission
to be human when we show up.

00:09:02.165 --> 00:09:04.265
When we hide under the
covers and feel like

00:09:04.265 --> 00:09:06.076
I have to be perfect,
I have to look perfect,

00:09:06.076 --> 00:09:07.224
I have to do perfect,

00:09:07.224 --> 00:09:09.712
then we rob each other of community.

00:09:09.712 --> 00:09:12.219
It's in our truth, in our imperfections,

00:09:12.219 --> 00:09:15.164
in our beauties, in our
flaws, in our strengths.

00:09:15.164 --> 00:09:17.684
That's where we find strength together.

00:09:17.684 --> 00:09:19.011
That's when we're honest.

00:09:19.011 --> 00:09:19.844
- Yes.

00:09:19.844 --> 00:09:21.118
- So, listen.

00:09:21.118 --> 00:09:24.086
I mean even, I know when
you look at Olivia Pope,

00:09:24.086 --> 00:09:25.650
you are seeing a team at work.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:29.011
I sat in that hair and
make-up chair for hours.

00:09:29.011 --> 00:09:31.373
You know, I didn't roll out
of bed to be Olivia Pope.

00:09:31.373 --> 00:09:34.287
There's somebody who gets
paid all day to brush my hair.

00:09:34.287 --> 00:09:35.994
So, you know, people,

00:09:35.994 --> 00:09:38.090
and I think it's important
for me to say that,

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:41.080
because it's a disservice
for you to feel like

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.021
you have to look like your
hair is laid all day long.

00:09:44.021 --> 00:09:47.069
Like literally, somebody
is in a hair union,

00:09:47.069 --> 00:09:49.311
following me around with
a brush and some hairspray

00:09:49.311 --> 00:09:52.058
all day so that Olivia
Pope's hair is laid.

00:09:52.058 --> 00:09:52.891
- And it is.

00:09:52.891 --> 00:09:55.380
- Well she's good, Linda's
good, Linda's good.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.652
I mean, you can't deny Linda's good.

00:09:57.652 --> 00:10:00.029
So that's, it's important
for us to talk about that,

00:10:00.029 --> 00:10:02.471
because I don't look like
that when I go to Whole Foods,

00:10:02.471 --> 00:10:03.304
you know what I mean?

00:10:03.304 --> 00:10:05.829
I don't bring Linda
with me to Whole Foods.

00:10:05.829 --> 00:10:07.427
I go to Whole Foods with
my little hair band,

00:10:07.427 --> 00:10:08.422
and it's up in a bun,

00:10:08.422 --> 00:10:10.438
and let's roll and get some apples.

00:10:10.438 --> 00:10:12.899
So I think it's important that we sort of

00:10:12.899 --> 00:10:15.837
frame our expectations, and know that,

00:10:15.837 --> 00:10:18.914
I know for me to show up as the character,

00:10:18.914 --> 00:10:20.537
I want to have all those pieces in place

00:10:20.537 --> 00:10:22.930
to inspire and to have a creative moment,

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:25.788
but I am not Olivia Pope,

00:10:25.788 --> 00:10:27.415
and I'm just Kerry.

00:10:27.415 --> 00:10:30.663
I was terrified to pursue
acting as a career.

00:10:30.663 --> 00:10:34.439
I really didn't think that
this was kind of an industry

00:10:34.439 --> 00:10:37.419
that I had what it takes to compete in.

00:10:37.419 --> 00:10:39.685
Because I didn't, and I don't necessarily

00:10:39.685 --> 00:10:41.071
have a drive toward fame.

00:10:41.071 --> 00:10:43.723
It's not something that
really calls to me,

00:10:43.723 --> 00:10:45.717
and so I just thought,
well I'll never make it,

00:10:45.717 --> 00:10:46.711
'cause I'm not one of those girls

00:10:46.711 --> 00:10:49.065
that wants to be on the
cover of every magazine,

00:10:49.065 --> 00:10:50.864
like I just really love to act.

00:10:50.864 --> 00:10:52.675
And it wasn't until I found out

00:10:52.675 --> 00:10:55.151
about the existence of actor's unions,

00:10:55.151 --> 00:10:57.763
and knowing what unions
mean in our country

00:10:57.763 --> 00:10:59.121
and in our history, I just thought,

00:10:59.121 --> 00:11:01.348
oh well, if there are unions for actors

00:11:01.348 --> 00:11:03.446
then that must mean there
are thousands of people

00:11:03.446 --> 00:11:05.859
who are making a living
doing what they love to do,

00:11:05.859 --> 00:11:07.279
even if they're never famous,

00:11:07.279 --> 00:11:09.280
so maybe I'll give it a try.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.180
- We're going to play dance charades,

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:15.176
and I'm going to give you
45 seconds to show me how,

00:11:15.176 --> 00:11:16.566
'cause I think you'll
be a very good dancer,

00:11:16.566 --> 00:11:18.396
and I like to play games.
- I like to dance.

00:11:18.396 --> 00:11:19.295
- So we'll go over here,

00:11:19.295 --> 00:11:22.629
and let's see what we have, over here.

00:11:22.629 --> 00:11:24.085
(applause)

00:11:24.085 --> 00:11:26.002
Move on the other side.

00:11:28.647 --> 00:11:29.666
- When you said dance for you

00:11:29.666 --> 00:11:30.795
I wasn't sure what you meant.

00:11:30.795 --> 00:11:33.215
- Yeah, no, no, I don't have
any dollar bills in my pants.

00:11:33.215 --> 00:11:34.563
- Okay, alright.

00:11:34.563 --> 00:11:35.848
I thought she was going to make it rain!

00:11:35.848 --> 00:11:36.681
- No, no.

00:11:36.681 --> 00:11:38.626
(laughs)

00:11:38.626 --> 00:11:39.941
Somebody get me some dollar bills.

00:11:39.941 --> 00:11:43.842
(laughter, applause)

00:11:43.842 --> 00:11:46.509
Alright, so you have 45
seconds on the clock.

00:11:46.509 --> 00:11:47.342
- Okay.

00:11:47.342 --> 00:11:48.175
- I'll hold up the card.

00:11:48.175 --> 00:11:50.810
I'm going to have to guess what you're...

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:51.733
- Okay, alright.

00:11:51.733 --> 00:11:52.566
- Ready?
- Yes.

00:11:52.566 --> 00:11:54.474
- 45 seconds on the clock, go.

00:11:54.474 --> 00:11:56.804
- Oh, okay, ready.

00:11:56.804 --> 00:11:57.637
- Gandnam Style.

00:11:57.637 --> 00:11:58.470
- Yes!

00:12:00.026 --> 00:12:00.859
- Vogue.

00:12:00.859 --> 00:12:01.692
- Yes!

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:07.177
- Waltzing, Flamenco dancing,

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:10.633
that is a kind of dance.

00:12:10.633 --> 00:12:11.494
- Yeah.

00:12:11.494 --> 00:12:12.327
- Yeah.

00:12:13.181 --> 00:12:15.354
It's ballroom dancing.

00:12:15.354 --> 00:12:16.764
And I pass.

00:12:16.764 --> 00:12:18.245
And it's Tango, damn.

00:12:18.245 --> 00:12:19.245
Sorry, 'kay.

00:12:21.322 --> 00:12:23.500
Oh, I should know the name of that.

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:24.333
Running man?

00:12:24.333 --> 00:12:25.166
- Uh-huh.

00:12:27.616 --> 00:12:28.449
- Tap?

00:12:32.156 --> 00:12:34.223
It's like clogging, or
Scottish dancing, or,

00:12:34.223 --> 00:12:35.056
- Yes.

00:12:35.056 --> 00:12:36.060
- Yeah something like that.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:37.243
Yeah, what's this.

00:12:37.243 --> 00:12:38.176
- Uh, okay.

00:12:38.176 --> 00:12:40.683
(buzzer buzzes)

00:12:40.683 --> 00:12:41.516
- Single ladies.

00:12:41.516 --> 00:12:42.507
- Yes!

00:12:42.507 --> 00:12:46.368
(applause, cheering)

00:12:46.368 --> 00:12:48.284
- You think that I'm bad.

00:12:48.284 --> 00:12:50.897
Peeples is in theaters now.

00:12:50.897 --> 00:12:53.215
Go see it, we'll be right back.

00:12:53.215 --> 00:12:56.600
(upbeat music)

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:00.312
- I'm here with the one and
only Kerry Washington right now,

00:13:00.312 --> 00:13:03.395
and we are about to play Box of Lies.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:07.453
Here's how it works.

00:13:07.453 --> 00:13:08.826
Upstage there are a bunch of boxes

00:13:08.826 --> 00:13:11.531
containing objects neither
of us have seen before.

00:13:11.531 --> 00:13:14.271
Taking turns, Kerry and I
are going to select a box

00:13:14.271 --> 00:13:16.143
and open it on our side of the table,

00:13:16.143 --> 00:13:18.064
out of the view of the other person.

00:13:18.064 --> 00:13:20.130
Once the object is out of the box,

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:23.054
you look at your opponent and
tell them what's in the box.

00:13:23.054 --> 00:13:25.533
You might be lying, you
might be telling the truth.

00:13:25.533 --> 00:13:27.015
Your opponent has to guess which one

00:13:27.015 --> 00:13:29.341
and say either lie, or truth.

00:13:29.341 --> 00:13:30.284
(laughter)

00:13:30.284 --> 00:13:31.651
If you guess correctly, you get a point.

00:13:31.651 --> 00:13:33.810
If you guess wrong, the
other person gets a point.

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:34.995
First to two points wins.

00:13:34.995 --> 00:13:39.337
Kerry, you're our guest, why
don't you pick your first box?

00:13:39.337 --> 00:13:40.601
- Thanks Jim.

00:13:40.601 --> 00:13:41.912
- You're welcome.

00:13:41.912 --> 00:13:44.829
(audience yelling)

00:13:50.183 --> 00:13:52.349
Oh, got a little heft to it.

00:13:52.349 --> 00:13:53.682
Hint number one.

00:13:58.736 --> 00:14:01.403
(Jimmy humming)

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:05.370
(laughter)

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:11.005
- In my box,

00:14:11.005 --> 00:14:13.255
(laughter)

00:14:19.083 --> 00:14:21.916
is a pumpkin with spaghetti in it.

00:14:26.983 --> 00:14:30.066
(laughter, clapping)

00:14:30.993 --> 00:14:32.426
- What?

00:14:32.426 --> 00:14:34.112
(laughs)

00:14:34.112 --> 00:14:35.606
Why are you laughing?

00:14:35.606 --> 00:14:37.939
Pumpkin with pasgetti on it.

00:14:39.124 --> 00:14:40.061
- Spaghetti.

00:14:40.061 --> 00:14:41.993
- Yeah, I wanted to see if you're acting.

00:14:41.993 --> 00:14:44.243
(laughter)

00:14:47.389 --> 00:14:48.222
You lie!

00:14:49.527 --> 00:14:50.547
- No!

00:14:50.547 --> 00:14:51.510
It's true!

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:52.910
- What?

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:53.743
(cheering)

00:14:53.743 --> 00:14:55.092
Let me see it.

00:14:55.092 --> 00:14:57.342
(applause)

00:14:59.071 --> 00:15:00.009
oh you're good, you're good.

00:15:00.009 --> 00:15:01.652
Let me pick one.

00:15:01.652 --> 00:15:04.569
(audience yelling)

00:15:06.083 --> 00:15:06.916
Three!

00:15:09.163 --> 00:15:11.176
- What's in the box, Jimmy?

00:15:11.176 --> 00:15:12.174
- Oh I don't know.

00:15:12.174 --> 00:15:15.128
- What's in your box, Jimmy?

00:15:15.128 --> 00:15:17.686
- Ah, well, well, well.

00:15:17.686 --> 00:15:18.519
- Yeah.

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:24.530
(laughter)

00:15:29.971 --> 00:15:32.304
- In my box, is an iPhone 6.

00:15:37.935 --> 00:15:39.518
- Plus, or regular?

00:15:42.327 --> 00:15:43.160
- Plus.

00:15:45.415 --> 00:15:50.381
With a figurine getting a
tattoo from another figurine,

00:15:50.381 --> 00:15:53.298
that I believe to be Justin Bieber.

00:15:54.921 --> 00:15:57.088
That is what is in my box.

00:16:03.684 --> 00:16:04.934
- That's a lie.

00:16:06.254 --> 00:16:07.445
- You got me.

00:16:07.445 --> 00:16:10.503
(cheering, applause)

00:16:10.503 --> 00:16:11.409
Wait, you won!

00:16:11.409 --> 00:16:12.847
It was that fast?

00:16:12.847 --> 00:16:14.171
You are too good at this.

00:16:14.171 --> 00:16:16.504
Kerry Washington, the champ!

00:16:19.424 --> 00:16:20.760
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:24.133
I made this video because
Delta Horse Races asked me to.

00:16:24.133 --> 00:16:25.253
So it there's a famous entrepreneur

00:16:25.253 --> 00:16:26.848
that you want me to profile next,

00:16:26.848 --> 00:16:28.067
leave it down in the comments below

00:16:28.067 --> 00:16:29.117
and I'll see what I can do.

00:16:29.117 --> 00:16:29.950
I'd also love to know,

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:32.166
what did she say that had
the biggest impact on you?

00:16:32.166 --> 00:16:34.846
What lesson was most
important to you and why?

00:16:34.846 --> 00:16:36.113
Please leave it in the comments

00:16:36.113 --> 00:16:37.833
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:16:37.833 --> 00:16:39.087
I also want to give a quick shout out to

00:16:39.087 --> 00:16:41.899
Jason Fonceca and Yuki,
thank you guys so much

00:16:41.899 --> 00:16:43.221
for going to the bookstore

00:16:43.221 --> 00:16:45.186
and picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word,

00:16:45.186 --> 00:16:47.557
it really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:16:47.557 --> 00:16:48.941
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:16:48.941 --> 00:16:50.112
I believe in you,

00:16:50.112 --> 00:16:52.463
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:16:52.463 --> 00:16:55.865
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:16:55.865 --> 00:16:57.365
I'll see you soon.

00:17:03.225 --> 00:17:05.392
- Your life is your story,

00:17:06.316 --> 00:17:08.154
and the adventure ahead of you

00:17:08.154 --> 00:17:12.443
is the journey to fulfill your
own purpose and potential.

00:17:12.443 --> 00:17:15.339
So I want to share a little
something with you about stories,

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:18.450
and about heroes, and
about how this all works.

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:19.951
It was here at GW that I first

00:17:19.951 --> 00:17:21.823
read the work of Joseph Campbell.

00:17:21.823 --> 00:17:23.559
And what attracted me to his writing

00:17:23.559 --> 00:17:25.900
was the concept of the hero's journey.

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:30.067
He wrote, "The hero's journey
always begins with the call.

00:17:31.154 --> 00:17:34.512
"One way or another a guide
must come and say look,

00:17:34.512 --> 00:17:36.773
"you're in sleepy land.

00:17:36.773 --> 00:17:39.156
"Awake, come on a trip.

00:17:39.156 --> 00:17:42.697
"There is an aspect of your
consciousness, your being,

00:17:42.697 --> 00:17:44.866
"that's not being touched.

00:17:44.866 --> 00:17:46.972
"And so it starts."

00:17:46.972 --> 00:17:50.475
This call to adventure
is what we call in film

00:17:50.475 --> 00:17:52.058
the inciting incident.

00:17:52.058 --> 00:17:55.536
It's an invitation out
of your comfort zone,

00:17:55.536 --> 00:17:57.401
and into your strength.

00:17:57.401 --> 00:18:01.215
Because by definition, being
a hero requires pushing beyond

00:18:01.215 --> 00:18:04.138
the bounds of what's comfortable.

00:18:04.138 --> 00:18:06.635
In 1996 in my junior year here,

00:18:06.635 --> 00:18:10.805
I had to push beyond the
bound of my comfort zone.

00:18:10.805 --> 00:18:13.415
The musical that year, which as you know,

00:18:13.415 --> 00:18:16.719
I was required to audition for,

00:18:16.719 --> 00:18:19.636
was entitled, Croak, The Last Frog.

00:18:22.242 --> 00:18:25.409
It was a beautiful musical about frogs

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:31.267
by GW's own Professor Leslie Jacobsen.

00:18:31.267 --> 00:18:32.100
Now,

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:33.735
(cheering)

00:18:33.735 --> 00:18:35.749
I have to be honest with you.

00:18:35.749 --> 00:18:39.832
It was not my lifetime
dream role to play a frog.

00:18:41.085 --> 00:18:44.464
In fact, the thought of it terrified me.

00:18:44.464 --> 00:18:47.021
Scared that the role
would be too difficult,

00:18:47.021 --> 00:18:48.818
and afraid of the embarrassment

00:18:48.818 --> 00:18:51.231
that would result from my failed attempts,

00:18:51.231 --> 00:18:53.616
I wondered if there was some way

00:18:53.616 --> 00:18:56.168
that I could get out of this audition.

00:18:56.168 --> 00:18:58.128
This is what Campbell describes as

00:18:58.128 --> 00:18:59.980
phase two of the hero's journey,

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:02.566
the refusal of the call.

00:19:02.566 --> 00:19:03.779
You've experienced it,

00:19:03.779 --> 00:19:05.992
an exciting opportunity presents itself,

00:19:05.992 --> 00:19:07.441
but our fear makes us believe

00:19:07.441 --> 00:19:10.024
that we're not capable
of meeting the challenge.

00:19:10.024 --> 00:19:12.505
There are 17 stages of the hero's journey

00:19:12.505 --> 00:19:14.400
as Campbell describes it but don't worry,

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:16.638
I'm not going to go through all of them.

00:19:16.638 --> 00:19:20.406
What I will tell you is
that I crossed the threshold

00:19:20.406 --> 00:19:22.706
into unknown territory, meaning,

00:19:22.706 --> 00:19:24.591
I showed up for my audition

00:19:24.591 --> 00:19:27.599
because I believed in the
message of the production.

00:19:27.599 --> 00:19:29.926
A message of love, and family,

00:19:29.926 --> 00:19:31.998
and environmental conservation.

00:19:31.998 --> 00:19:34.163
I wanted to support Leslie Jacobsen,

00:19:34.163 --> 00:19:36.702
my brilliant professor and my mentor,

00:19:36.702 --> 00:19:39.840
and I had to fulfill my
scholarship requirements,

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:42.123
because, as many of
you probably relate to,

00:19:42.123 --> 00:19:45.827
I wanted to complete
my degree and graduate.

00:19:45.827 --> 00:19:47.744
So I answered the call.

00:19:48.582 --> 00:19:50.694
I pushed past my comfort zone,

00:19:50.694 --> 00:19:53.325
and when I did, I won the role.

00:19:53.325 --> 00:19:56.575
So now I was in the belly of the whale.

00:19:57.459 --> 00:20:01.296
Now I had to be willing
to be the lead frog.

00:20:01.296 --> 00:20:03.753
Again, I was terrified.

00:20:03.753 --> 00:20:05.862
This was a serious musical

00:20:05.862 --> 00:20:07.562
with important dramatic themes.

00:20:07.562 --> 00:20:09.494
How could I commit to the role

00:20:09.494 --> 00:20:10.909
and bring life to the character

00:20:10.909 --> 00:20:13.768
in a way that made people think and feel,

00:20:13.768 --> 00:20:16.866
rather than giggle and mock us.

00:20:16.866 --> 00:20:20.155
So I approached the role
as a social scientist,

00:20:20.155 --> 00:20:21.883
the way GW taught me to.

00:20:21.883 --> 00:20:25.292
I thought, I'm going to study frogs.

00:20:25.292 --> 00:20:27.593
I went to the zoo up in Woodley Park

00:20:27.593 --> 00:20:30.694
and I spent hours watching frogs.

00:20:30.694 --> 00:20:33.478
I read about frogs, I held frogs,

00:20:33.478 --> 00:20:35.755
I watched frog documentaries,

00:20:35.755 --> 00:20:38.011
I figured out ways to physicalize

00:20:38.011 --> 00:20:39.246
my performance as a frog

00:20:39.246 --> 00:20:41.427
without seeming to be a cartoon.

00:20:41.427 --> 00:20:44.291
I faced my fears and I stepped out onto

00:20:44.291 --> 00:20:46.547
that Marvin Center stage,

00:20:46.547 --> 00:20:48.042
(cheering)

00:20:48.042 --> 00:20:51.516
and it became a role that
transformed my thinking

00:20:51.516 --> 00:20:56.027
about how I use my body to do
what I do every single day,

00:20:56.027 --> 00:20:59.606
and more importantly, to this day,

00:20:59.606 --> 00:21:03.773
it is my father's most
favorite thing I've ever done.

00:21:06.151 --> 00:21:07.921
I'm serious, you can ask him.

00:21:07.921 --> 00:21:09.805
(applause)

00:21:09.805 --> 00:21:13.972
But the lesson here isn't
how you too, can be a frog,

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:18.021
the lesson is that you
are sitting here today

00:21:18.021 --> 00:21:22.021
because you too, learned
how to answer the call.

00:21:23.062 --> 00:21:26.950
Your degree is proof that
you have what it takes

00:21:26.950 --> 00:21:30.019
to take the leap into the adventure

00:21:30.019 --> 00:21:32.387
of your unique experience.

00:21:32.387 --> 00:21:35.813
You don't earn a degree
by doing, and being,

00:21:35.813 --> 00:21:38.157
and existing in the comfort zone

00:21:38.157 --> 00:21:40.577
of what you already know.

00:21:40.577 --> 00:21:43.832
So whether it's taken two years or seven

00:21:43.832 --> 00:21:45.314
to get today's degree,

00:21:45.314 --> 00:21:47.922
I want you to look back on the journey

00:21:47.922 --> 00:21:49.525
that brought you here.

00:21:49.525 --> 00:21:52.963
What moments challenged you most?

00:21:52.963 --> 00:21:55.960
When were you asked to step outside of

00:21:55.960 --> 00:21:57.973
your familiar territory in order to

00:21:57.973 --> 00:22:00.987
rise to the occasion of your potential?

00:22:00.987 --> 00:22:03.904
How have you pushed past your fear?

00:22:05.132 --> 00:22:06.983
I want you to remember those moments

00:22:06.983 --> 00:22:09.557
because they will embolden you,

00:22:09.557 --> 00:22:13.956
and affirm you, and remind
you that you did this.

00:22:13.956 --> 00:22:16.035
You completed this journey.

00:22:16.035 --> 00:22:18.206
You answered the call.

00:22:18.206 --> 00:22:21.252
And now you can continue to do so,

00:22:21.252 --> 00:22:23.018
but the choice is yours.

00:22:23.018 --> 00:22:24.236
When you leave here today

00:22:24.236 --> 00:22:27.137
and commence the next stage of your life,

00:22:27.137 --> 00:22:30.037
you can follow someone else's script,

00:22:30.037 --> 00:22:33.157
try to make choices that
will make other people happy,

00:22:33.157 --> 00:22:36.387
avoid discomfort, do what is expected,

00:22:36.387 --> 00:22:38.216
and copy the status quo.

00:22:38.216 --> 00:22:42.947
Or, you can look at all that
you have accomplished today,

00:22:42.947 --> 00:22:46.608
and use it as fuel to venture forth

00:22:46.608 --> 00:22:48.691
and write your own story.

00:22:49.716 --> 00:22:53.216
If you do, amazing things will take shape.

